NAME
       top - show the busiest processes and the system's load, updated live

SYNOPSIS
       top [-b] [-n COUNT] [-d SECONDS] [-u USER] [-p PID]

DESCRIPTION
       top fills the terminal with a summary of the system and a table of
       processes, refreshed every few seconds, the busiest first. Use it to
       find a process that eats CPU or memory, then deal with it (renice it
       or kill it).

   The summary lines
       top -    the time, the uptime, the number of users and the load
                average over 1, 5 and 15 minutes (the number of processes
                wanting a CPU; compare it with the number of CPUs).
       Tasks:   how many processes exist and in which state.
       %Cpu(s): how the CPU time is spent: us user programs, sy the kernel,
                ni niced programs, id idle, wa waiting for disks.
       MiB Mem, MiB Swap:
                total, free, used and cache memory, and swap use.

   The columns
       PID      the process ID (for kill and renice).
       USER     the owner.
       PR, NI   the scheduling priority and the nice value (-20 to 19;
                higher is nicer, so it gets less CPU).
       VIRT, RES, SHR
                virtual, resident (really in RAM) and shared memory, in KiB.
       S        the state: R running, S sleeping, D waiting for a disk,
                T stopped, Z zombie.
       %CPU, %MEM
                share of one CPU and of the memory.
       TIME+    CPU time used so far.
       COMMAND  the program.

INTERACTIVE KEYS
       P      sort by CPU use (the default).

       M      sort by memory use.

       k      kill a process: top asks for the PID and the signal.

       r      renice a process: top asks for the PID and the new nice value.

       q      quit.

OPTIONS
       -b     Batch mode: print plain text instead of a live screen, for
              saving to a file or piping.

       -n COUNT
              Stop after COUNT updates (top -b -n 1 prints one snapshot).

       -d SECONDS
              Time between updates.

       -u USER
              Show only USER's processes.

       -p PID Show only these processes.

EXAMPLES
       Look at the system interactively (press q to leave):

           top

       Save one snapshot of the process table:

           top -b -n 1 > /root/top.txt

       After finding a busy process with top, lower its priority or end it:

           renice -n 10 -p 2345
           kill 2345

NOTES
       ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head gives a one-off list sorted the same way.

SEE ALSO
       ps(1), kill(1), renice(1), nice(1), uptime(1), free(1)
